# Proposed MHS Internal Medicine Curriculum (4+1 Model)

This schedule reflects an **X+Y (4+1)** alternating block model. In this structure, the academic year consists of ten 5-week cycles per year. For each cycle, residents spend 4 weeks on a core rotation, followed immediately by 1 week dedicated entirely to continuity clinic. 
*   **Total Cycle:** 10 core blocks (40 weeks) + 10 clinic weeks (10 weeks) + 2 weeks standard vacation/holiday = 52 weeks.
*   **Continuity Structure:** For the 2026-2027 academic year, the **PGY-2 class** will complete their "+1" continuity clinic blocks at **NIMA**, remaining at this site to finish out their residency. The **PGY-1** and **current PGY-3** classes are assigned to **CCIM** for their longitudinal continuity site.

In addition to the 5th-week clinic, residents will cycle through dedicated month-long (4-week) ambulatory rotations (such as completing a month in the NIMA clinic) to satisfy ACGME general outpatient requirements and provide staffing support.

---

## PGY-1: Intern Year
**Theme: Foundational Patient Care & Skill Acquisition**
Interns focus heavily on direct inpatient management, primary care foundations, and initial triage skills. *Note: Emergency Medicine is strictly limited to PGY2/3.*

| Blocks (4-weeks each) | Rotation Types | Specific Rotations Used |
| :---: | :--- | :--- |
| **4** | **General Inpatient Wards** | Wards B, Wards R, Wards Metro |
| **2** | **Critical Care (Main / Metro)** | ICU Main or ICU Metro |
| **1** | **Split: Night Float / Subspecialty** | 2 weeks Night Float (NF) + 2 weeks Subspecialty Elective |
| **3** | **Electives / Subspecialties / Vacation** | E.g., Cardiology, Infectious Disease; Includes Intern Jeopardy Assignment |

---

## PGY-2: Junior Resident Year
**Theme: Supervisory Roles, Advanced Triage, & Ambulatory Immersion**
The PGY-2 takes on team leadership on Wards and gains intensive triage exposure via Emergency Medicine. *Note: CVICU and NIMA are available strictly as electives, whereas Metro ICU can be actively assigned as a core rotation.*

| Blocks (4-weeks each) | Rotation Types | Specific Rotations Used |
| :---: | :--- | :--- |
| **3** | **Supervisory Wards** | Wards B, Wards R, Wards Metro |
| **1** | **Critical Care Core** | ICU Main |
| **1** | **Emergency Medicine** | Emergency Medicine (EM) *(Can be split into two 2-week blocks)* |
| **1** | **Split: Night Float / Elective** | 2 weeks Supervisory Night Float (NF) + 2 weeks Elective (e.g., CVICU) |
| **1** | **Mandatory Geriatrics** | Geriatrics |
| **1** | **Split: Mandatory Consults** | 2 weeks Neurology + 2 weeks Gastroenterology |
| **1** | **Split: Mandatory Consults** | 2 weeks Pulmonology + 2 weeks Nephrology |
| **1** | **Subspecialty / Elective / Vacation** | Additional Consults, Board Prep, Vacation |

---

## PGY-3: Senior Resident Year
**Theme: Autonomy & Individualized Education**
Senior residents polish their ward management, engage with their final rigorous critical care duties (two mandatory 4-week ICU blocks), and dedicate immense time to individualized rotations preparing for their specific career path.

| Blocks (4-weeks each) | Rotation Types | Specific Rotations Used |
| :---: | :--- | :--- |
| **1** | **Senior Supervisory Wards** | Wards B, Wards R, Wards Metro |
| **1** | **Junior Hospitalist** | 1-on-1 with Attending (Acts as Senior Jeopardy) |
| **2** | **Critical Care Core** | ICU Main or ICU Metro (Resident's Choice) |
| **1** | **Hospice & Palliative Care** | Hospice and Palliative Care |
| **1** | **Addiction Medicine** | Addiction Medicine |
| **1** | **Staffing Assignment: NIMA** | 4 solid weeks assigned to NIMA |
| **2** | **Subspecialty / Individualized** | Endocrinology, Rheumatology, Heme-Onc, CVICU, Board Prep |
| **1** | **Split: Night Float / Elective** | 2 weeks Night Float (NF) + 2 weeks Elective |

---

## Jeopardy & Backup Coverage
To maintain the required 4+1 structure and preserve inpatient caps without compromising continuity clinics or required subspecialty blocks, Jeopardy coverage is strictly defined to avoid utilizing the ambulatory "+1" cohort:
*   **Senior Jeopardy:** Covered by the PGY-3 residents assigned to the *Junior Hospitalist* block. If pulled to cover the ICU or Wards, the supervising attending assumes primary responsibility for their hospitalist patients.
*   **Intern Jeopardy:** Handled by a PGY-1 currently assigned to a flexible *Elective/Consult* block.

